  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1039 PM CDT SAT SEP 19 2026\n\nWe had a fairly active afternoon unfold across the region as\nseveral diurnally driven storms overperformed at times. Several\nstorms produced damaging wind gusts that led to downed trees and\npowerlines across Central Alabama. Activity has waned over the\nlast few hours as we have lost daytime heating. Low level\nmoisture left in the wake of these storms will likely lead to\npatchy fog developing around sunrise Sunday morning. Water vapor\nimagery reveals our well advertised upper level closed low\nemerging over the eastern Gulf as it moves off the coast of\nFlorida. A plume of moisture associated with this feature will\ndrive our weather as we wrap up the weekend. Another round of\nscattered showers and thunderstorms are expected Sunday\nafternoon. Strong wind gusts will be the primary threat with this\nactivity. The upper level low will begin to break down early next\nweek, leaving healthy moisture in place across the state. This\nwill allow for low to medium chances for diurnally driven\nconvection daily through Wednesday. By the middle of the week, an\nupper trough will swing across the Plains while sending a cold\nfront our way. This will provide a decent \"cool\" down for us with\nhigh settling into the mid 80s. The more noticeable change will\ncome during the morning hours as we will see temps in the low to\nmid 60s by Friday morning. This was the first time in quite some\ntime that I remember looking at the ECMWF EFI and not seeing a\nsignal for anomalously warm temps! With that said, we will have\nto get through a few more days of highs in the mid 90s before our\n\"relief\" arrives.\n\n95/Castillo",
